commit e8a16e2e90abc7395871ab89c917c60e79bbc3a5
Author: Ivan Oparin <oparin@me.com>
Date:   Tue Aug 25 07:49:38 2026 +0400

    Copy the broker wrapper on Windows instead of hard-linking it
    
    Windows reported access denied when the tests cleaned up their temporary
    directory: the wrapper was hard-linked to the running manager executable,
    so the operating system saw the same locked file identity and refused to
    unlink it. The assertions had already passed; only the deferred cleanup
    failed, which is exactly the shape that hides a lifecycle defect behind a
    green-looking test body.
    
    Materialize the wrapper as an independent byte copy on Windows and return
    only once both handles are closed. Unix keeps the hard-link fast path,
    where no such identity is shared. Both broker tests now materialize
    through the production path and remove the wrapper before cleanup, so the
    lifecycle is asserted rather than assumed.

diff --git a/internal/buildrepo/httpsbroker.go b/internal/buildrepo/httpsbroker.go
index 6c35cc4..be40a67 100644
--- a/internal/buildrepo/httpsbroker.go
+++ b/internal/buildrepo/httpsbroker.go
@@ -159,8 +159,14 @@ func materializeHTTPSCredentialBroker(root, executable string, credentials HTTPS
 }
 
 func copyBrokerExecutable(source, destination string) (err error) {
-	if err := os.Link(source, destination); err == nil {
-		return nil
+	// A Windows hard link still names the running manager executable. Windows
+	// keeps that file identity locked until the manager exits, so a temporary
+	// wrapper created this way cannot be removed when the fetch workspace is
+	// cleaned up. Materialize independent bytes on Windows instead.
+	if runtime.GOOS != "windows" {
+		if err := os.Link(source, destination); err == nil {
+			return nil
+		}
 	}
 	input, err := os.Open(source) // #nosec G304 -- admitted absolute manager executable.
 	if err != nil {
